//? Title: Paired categorical tables and stratified odds ratios
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare paired margins and transitions

Rows record the initial category for each person and columns record
the follow-up category. Stuart–Maxwell tests whether the total number
of people in each category changed. Bowker tests whether each
transition is as common as its reverse. In this example the marginal
totals are identical, with nine people moving along each step of
the cycle A to B to C to A.

```{cpp, id=paired-table-tests, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/table_symmetry.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>> counts{{2, 9, 0}, {0, 2, 9}, {9, 0, 2}};
    std::println("| Test | Chi-square | Degrees of freedom | p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& result : {stuart_maxwell_test(counts), bowker_test(counts)}) {
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.0f} | {:.4g} |", result.method,
                     result.statistic, result.parameter1, result.p_value);
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Test | Chi-square | Degrees of freedom | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Stuart–Maxwell marginal homogeneity test | 0.000 | 2 | 1 |
| Bowker symmetry test | 27.000 | 3 | 5.887e-06 |
// result_end

> Check a common odds ratio across strata

Four independent clinics compare event rates in two groups. Each row of
the input lists events and nonevents in the first group, then events and
nonevents in the second group. Breslow–Day compares the observed counts
with the counts fitted at a common Mantel–Haenszel odds ratio. Tarone's
adjustment accounts for estimating that odds ratio from the same strata.

```{cpp, id=common-odds-ratio, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/table_symmetry.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<std::array<std::size_t, 4>> clinics{
        {8, 2, 4, 6}, {5, 3, 2, 6}, {13, 2, 3, 17}, {0, 9, 4, 8}
    };
    const auto adjusted = breslow_day_test(clinics);
    const auto raw = breslow_day_test(clinics, false);
    std::println("The fitted common odds ratio is {:.3f}.", adjusted.estimate1);
    std::println("");
    std::println("| Test | Chi-square | Degrees of freedom | p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& result : {raw, adjusted}) {
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.0f} | {:.4g} |", result.method,
                     result.statistic, result.parameter1, result.p_value);
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The fitted common odds ratio is 3.980.

| Test | Chi-square | Degrees of freedom | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Breslow–Day test | 17.048 | 3 | 0.000691 |
| Tarone-adjusted Breslow–Day test | 16.769 | 3 | 0.0007883 |
// result_end

